# Workflow

```text
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → CI green → human review → squash merge → retrospective
```

1. Grill when the API, the meaning, or a roadmap boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and `docs/design.ja.md` settles the question.
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off.
3. A change to the meaning of the design is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`).
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. `docs/roadmap.md` keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only. A spike's result goes into the `## Result` section of the Issue body (`gh issue edit --body-file`) before the PR is called ready. The PR also sets its roadmap row to `done` and points **今** at the next row.
5. Branch from `main`: `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`, for example `feat/12-select-joins`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type.
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. Use `.github/pull_request_template.md` (`.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`). `Closes #N` stands alone on a line under `## Related` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Before calling a PR ready, and before answering a review, read the checks of the pushed head (`gh pr checks <n>`). A PR that shows no checks is first checked for a conflict with `main` (`gh pr view <n> --json mergeable`).
7. A human reviews and squash-merges. An agent merges only when the owner names the PR and orders it, after the checks pass, with one message in the form of `.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc`.
8. After the merge, delete the local and remote branch.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Issue templates and labels

Labels have the form `<axis>:<value>`. The only axis today is `type`.

| Template   | Label           | Commit / PR type           |
| ---------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `type:feat`     | `feat`                     |
| `spike`    | `type:spike`    | `chore` (code is scratch)  |
| `bug`      | `type:bug`      | `fix`                      |
| `refactor` | `type:refactor` | `refactor`                 |
| `test`     | `type:test`     | `test`                     |
| `design`   | `type:docs`     | `docs`                     |
| `task`     | `type:chore`    | `chore` / `build` / `ci`   |

## Gates

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) is the merge gate. Every check that CI can run is in CI: a check that runs only on a developer's machine is invisible to reviewers and can be skipped without anyone knowing, so it guarantees nothing.

- `./build.ps1` on Windows, Linux, and macOS: tool restore, CSharpier check, restore, then Release and Debug builds with warnings as errors, each followed by every `tests/**/*.Tests.csproj`.
- Database integration tests run in CI against service containers of every supported database (`.cursor/rules/containers.mdc`). Locally they run against WSL containers while developing; that run is a note in the PR, not the gate.
- The NativeAOT publish check runs in CI (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`).
- A check that cannot run in CI is named in the Issue with the reason, and its result goes under `## Verification`.
- After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch.
- Treating a roadmap title, or a file under `.dev/`, as permission to code.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill.
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an order that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR.
- Moving a CI check to "run it locally" to get a PR through.

## Numbers

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere: a branch name, a commit, a PR body. Issue and PR numbers share one sequence; the next one is not predictable.

```text
# BAD — write `Closes #8` into the body, then create the Issue, which gets #9
# GOOD — create the Issue, read its number from the output, then write the body
```

## Stacked PRs

A second Issue that builds on an open PR gets its own branch and PR based on the first. A stacked PR is merged only after its base has merged. When the base merges, retarget the next PR to `main` first, then delete the merged branch.

## Retrospective

After a squash merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, or `docs/design.ja.md`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on is a lesson: how a dialect behaves, a way a tool fails on this setup, a mistake the review or the owner had to catch.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that comes next. Postponing it needs a roadmap row (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).

Then, without waiting for an ask, file a `chore` Issue and one PR that writes each lesson into the file it names. The next feature branch waits until that PR is open.
